.TH CHTTPCLIENT_SET_TLS 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_set_tls \- set the TLS configuration of a client
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_set_tls(chttpcli cli,
                                  const chttp_tls_config_t *tls);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function copies the TLS configuration that you give into the
client. A tls of NULL puts the defaults back. Those defaults are
verification of the chain and of the hostname, the system CA bundle and no
client certificate.

The function deep-copies the strings of chttp_tls_config_t into
buffers that the client owns. Those strings are cert_path, key_path
and ca_bundle_path. The caller can free or reuse its own originals at
once after this call returns.

This function does not check that it can read cert_path, key_path and
ca_bundle_path. The client checks each path later, at the moment an
HTTPS request needs it. A path can point at no file at all, or at a
directory or another object that is not a file. This function accepts
such a path and reports no error. That path then gives
ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed from chttpclient_do or from
chttpclient_do_streaming, after a request needs it.

The client reads each of the three files whole. It rejects a file
above 16 MB with ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed, and does not read it
into memory. A PEM artefact is much smaller than that. A certificate
or a key is a few kilobytes, and a full system CA bundle is a few
hundred kilobytes. Only a path that points at something other than a
PEM file reaches the limit.

ca_bundle_path must also give at least one certificate. A file that
the client can read can still parse to no certificate. An empty file,
a key on its own, and a file that is not PEM at all are such files.
The client reports ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed for them. It does
not accept a trust store that trusts no issuer.

ca_bundle_path may also hold certificate revocation lists (CRLs)
beside its CA certificates. The client then enforces them against the
certificate chain of every server, as the ssl_crl directive of nginx
does: every certificate of that chain below its trust anchor needs a
CRL of its issuer in the bundle. A request fails with
ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed for a revoked certificate, for a
certificate whose issuer has no CRL in the bundle, and for a CRL past
its next update time. A bundle with no CRL checks no revocation.

cert_path and key_path are a pair. If you set one of the two and leave
the other NULL, the function gives ccol_invalid_args. It does not
treat that state as "no client certificate". Such silence leaves an
mTLS deployment in the belief that it presents a client certificate,
while it never does.

A chttp_tls_config_t whose every byte is zero is the SAFE
configuration: it verifies the certificate chain of the server and
matches its hostname. Both verification fields are named for what
turning them ON gives up, so a struct built with = {0}, or with a
designated initializer that names only some of the fields, can never
end up with less verification than the caller asked for.

insecure_skip_verify accepts any certificate from any peer, and it also
makes insecure_skip_hostname_check irrelevant. A hostname matched
against a certificate that nothing validated proves nothing, so there is
no way to ask for the hostname check without the chain check.

insecure_skip_hostname_check keeps the chain verification and drops only
the hostname match. See CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT(3) for more.

The new configuration applies to every request after this call
returns. This includes a request that could reuse a keep-alive
connection from the pool. The key of both idle pools is the origin. An
origin is "scheme://host:port", or "unix://<path>". That key says
nothing about the TLS policy of the handshake of a pooled connection.
The client therefore retires every pooled HTTPS connection that it
made under the configuration that this call replaces, and does not
reuse it. The next request to that origin does a fresh handshake under
the new settings. The client keeps reusing a pooled plain-HTTP
connection, because no TLS setting applies to it. Another request can
already hold a connection out of the pool. That connection finishes
under the configuration that it started with. The client retires only
the connections that are still idle.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli   The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
tls   The TLS configuration to copy, or NULL to put the defaults
      back.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success          The client has the new configuration.
ccol_invalid_args     cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale handle of
                      a client that is already destroyed, or you set
                      exactly one of tls->cert_path and
                      tls->key_path, or you set
                      tls->insecure_skip_verify together with a
                      tls->ca_bundle_path. A bundle exists to be
                      verified against, so that pair states two
                      incompatible policies and the function refuses
                      it rather than picking one.
ccol_not_enough_memory
                      An allocation of memory failed. The TLS
                      configuration of the client then holds the
                      defaults (verification of the chain and of
                      the hostname, the system CA bundle and no
                      client certificate). The client does not apply
                      a part of your configuration.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT (3),
.BR ccol_create_chttpclient (3)
